Recently, I was in New York.

42
00:04:33,206 --> 00:04:48,514
A lot of Himalayan people have now moved to New York, and many of them were born in America and grew up in America.

43
00:04:50,635 --> 00:04:52,476
One of my nephews,

44
00:04:55,202 --> 00:04:56,663
He was born in America.

45
00:04:58,064 --> 00:05:02,448
He went to school and grew up in America.

46
00:05:03,629 --> 00:05:10,515
And recently he visited Tibet, Lhasa and all the places.

47
00:05:12,336 --> 00:05:16,720
So, out of curiosity, I told him,

48
00:05:20,896 --> 00:05:27,098
If you were to ask a question after visiting Tibet, what would you ask?

49
00:05:28,859 --> 00:05:31,400
He is a young Himalayan teenage boy.

50
00:05:33,612 --> 00:05:36,334
They have this funny kind of accent.

51
00:05:36,494 --> 00:05:42,917
I don't know if it's all the Himalayan, Tibetan or all American, I don't know.

52
00:05:43,198 --> 00:05:44,298
A little bit funny accent.

53
00:05:45,759 --> 00:05:48,821
But I would ask a question.

54
00:05:51,222 --> 00:05:53,964
It doesn't work very well when it's so loud.

55
00:05:54,844 --> 00:05:56,966
I can imitate better without the microphone.

56
00:05:57,686 --> 00:05:58,887
Anyway, the question was...

57
00:06:00,136 --> 00:06:04,519
Why didn't we industrialize, industrialize, industrialize?

58
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:05,420
Huh?

59
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:07,141
Industrialize.

60
00:06:08,222 --> 00:06:10,163
Industrialize in Tibet.

61
00:06:10,823 --> 00:06:11,784
That was the question.

62
00:06:13,425 --> 00:06:26,334
Because in the West, if you look and travel around, you see so many from a few hundred years, we have all these factories building so many

63
00:06:29,785 --> 00:06:30,846
what you call machines.

64
00:06:32,728 --> 00:06:34,809
Recently, I went to Niagara Falls.

65
00:06:35,750 --> 00:06:42,276
I saw the museum of Nikola Tesla, how the electricity was invented.

66
00:06:42,296 --> 00:06:43,837
I saw all those things.

67
00:06:46,279 --> 00:07:00,183
Interesting question from a young Tibetan who was sort of born in America, brought up, goes back to Tibet, and sees that, oh, there's no industry, never developed.

68
00:07:01,764 --> 00:07:07,265
So my answer is, it doesn't mean that we are kind of backward.

69
00:07:08,306 --> 00:07:13,167
We didn't focus outward industry.

70
00:07:14,847 --> 00:07:15,808
Outward development.

71
00:07:16,929 --> 00:07:19,631
We focus more towards inward development.

72
00:07:21,973 --> 00:07:42,528
I think, I don't know the timing exactly, but when in the West, when they are building, creating electricity, and I don't know, trying to fly, all those things, around the same time, Longchen Ramjam in Tibet, writing the Seven Treasures,

73
00:07:44,677 --> 00:07:46,500
how to develop inner peace.

74
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,444
So the focus went two different way.

75
00:07:51,426 --> 00:08:01,480
You end up with aeroplane and cars, we end up with the precious practice, how to practice Dharma, how to overcome your negative emotions.

76
00:08:03,158 --> 00:08:05,380
So this is two ways we went.

77
00:08:05,860 --> 00:08:07,401
And now it sort of comes together.

78
00:08:07,421 --> 00:08:11,224
Llamas can take the aeroplane, come here and give the teaching.

79
00:08:12,806 --> 00:08:15,067
So actually it worked out quite well.

80
00:08:18,030 --> 00:08:31,560
But anyway, no matter how much you have outer development, as many of you now realize, and many of you live in the West, grew up in the West, you see,

81
00:08:32,301 --> 00:08:41,549
then no matter how much outer development, it doesn't really give you total inner peace or fullest satisfaction of life.

82
00:08:45,763 --> 00:08:51,548
Recently I was in Nepal, up in the Himalaya, in a place called Manang.

83
00:08:53,429 --> 00:08:56,471
Some people call it Nishang, Manang.

84
00:08:56,571 --> 00:09:03,597
It is very near to Tibet, very similar to an environment like Tibet.

85
00:09:05,262 --> 00:09:15,189
The place where Milarepa meditated, the place where Milarepa met the hunter, the famous story.

86
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:17,430
The road was terrible.

87
00:09:18,711 --> 00:09:20,212
You can hardly call it a road.

88
00:09:21,233 --> 00:09:23,215
It was a really scary, scary road.

89
00:09:24,755 --> 00:09:37,819
Even though the journey was a little bit tough, but once I reached there, it was so beautiful, just in front of the Annapurna mountain, very peaceful.

90
00:09:39,259 --> 00:09:48,062
And now there is electricity and some basic necessities are there, but still people live very, very simple life.

91
00:09:50,078 --> 00:09:56,925
And just below the Melarepa cave, there was a small hermitage.

92
00:09:57,947 --> 00:10:00,129
And there was one Kempo doing retreat.

93
00:10:01,230 --> 00:10:03,833
I believe he's from Jigong Gaji tradition.

94
00:10:06,536 --> 00:10:08,258
And I asked how long he's been there.

95
00:10:09,659 --> 00:10:12,022
For six years, he's been doing retreat.

96
00:10:13,399 --> 00:10:17,441
And I believe he chose to sit there and meditate.

97
00:10:18,682 --> 00:10:22,944
Very minimum, basic kind of necessity.

98
00:10:24,044 --> 00:10:28,066
There is electricity, and a very, very basic necessity.

99
00:10:28,687 --> 00:10:34,029
But very happy, very happy, very content life.

100
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:37,011
Then I compare.

101
00:10:39,022 --> 00:10:41,906
New York, you have this skyscraper.

102
00:10:43,187 --> 00:10:54,020
I'm not going to tell who, but someone with a golden toilet, golden sink where he can wash his face.

103
00:10:54,061 --> 00:10:56,684
24 hours angry, tweeting angry notes.

104
00:10:59,282 --> 00:11:00,703
I'm sure you can guess who he is.

105
00:11:01,863 --> 00:11:04,624
I'm not going to mention name because I have to go back someday.

106
00:11:04,664 --> 00:11:05,745
I might not get visa.

107
00:11:08,566 --> 00:11:09,606
See the comparison.

108
00:11:10,687 --> 00:11:18,030
The guy in the Himalaya choose to live in a very simple basicness city, very happy.

109
00:11:19,434 --> 00:11:20,695
and continues to practice.

110
00:11:22,095 --> 00:11:32,200
Similar in one of these towers, Italian furniture, golden toilet, 24 hours angry, not happy.

111
00:11:34,689 --> 00:11:57,958
So therefore, I think what our great kings, like Khenlop Chuesom, King Trisong Detsen, Guru Rinpoche, Khenchen Bodhisattva, invited lots of scholars, translated Dharma texts into Tibet around 7th, 8th century.

112
00:12:00,657 --> 00:12:11,040
It was a kind of national project of Tibet to bring the Dharma and translate.

113
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:22,203
I think when you look around in the world these days, people talk about, I don't know, 20% of the income is invested in weapons,

114
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:46,757
and military but during that time tibet they are not looking really looking for interest to the development they are trying to bring the authentic teachings from india national treasure a national budget i think we can say it was spent on bringing dharma into tibet and translating

115
00:12:47,677 --> 00:13:03,827
And not only that, from that time till our masters, and of course His Holiness, which is still alive, till our great masters, they managed to preserve that.

116
00:13:05,928 --> 00:13:15,294
It is said that maybe around 10 to 20% of the Tibetan population are monks and nuns practicing.

117
00:13:16,515 --> 00:13:16,715
So,

118
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:18,860
you get good results.

119
00:13:22,362 --> 00:13:33,485
Out of the whole country, 20% of the population practice trying to achieve some kind of inner development, inner realization.

120
00:13:34,006 --> 00:13:35,566
Then you get those great masters.

121
00:13:42,396 --> 00:13:52,921
From the 7th century till our time, we focus not on outer development, not focusing on outer industrialization, but inner development.

122
00:13:54,522 --> 00:13:56,263
How to overcome negative emotions?

123
00:14:01,900 --> 00:14:05,521
how to achieve an awakened state of mind, enlightenment.

124
00:14:06,501 --> 00:14:07,082
So like that.

125
00:14:08,382 --> 00:14:12,483
So that's the answer I gave to him.

126
00:14:12,503 --> 00:14:19,525
I think our younger generations should realize that we are not sort of ...

127
00:14:19,925 --> 00:14:28,328
Even though we don't have that much outer development, we don't have that much skyscrapers, but we have many, many realized masters

128
00:14:29,672 --> 00:14:44,243
And many of them, around 1959, the situation in Tibet, they flee Tibet, came out and then spread the Dharma, Buddha Dharma.

129
00:14:47,306 --> 00:14:48,567
And then, I think,

130
00:14:52,008 --> 00:15:00,452
Many of our younger generation, sometimes people ask, Oh, what are you?

131
00:15:01,532 --> 00:15:03,814
And they say, Oh, I'm Buddhist.

132
00:15:04,754 --> 00:15:07,435
I'm born to a Buddhist family, so I'm Buddhist.

133
00:15:08,716 --> 00:15:13,858
So this, I want to tell you that you cannot be born as a Buddhist.

134
00:15:14,639 --> 00:15:17,840
It does not exist in Buddhism.

135
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:19,761
You become Buddhist.

136
00:15:21,796 --> 00:15:24,299
by understanding what the Buddha's teaching is.

137
00:15:28,385 --> 00:15:37,236
I believe that if you're Jewish, if your mother is a Jew, then you become a Jew.

138
00:15:40,930 --> 00:15:45,935
Similarly, Hindus, if your father is a Hindu, you become a Hindu.

139
00:15:47,116 --> 00:15:52,883
But I was wondering, if your father is Hindu and mother is Jew, you are in big trouble.

140
00:15:56,130 --> 00:15:56,811
That's a joke.

141
00:15:57,731 --> 00:16:02,955
Anyway, we don't have that kind of system.

142
00:16:04,356 --> 00:16:11,662
So you cannot, just because you are born to a Buddhist father and Buddhist mother, you become Buddhist.

143
00:16:15,859 --> 00:16:33,251
In the teachings, when they talk about precious human life, we always make an aspiration, may I reborn next life to parents who practice dharma, and may I be able to enter into dharma.

144
00:16:33,892 --> 00:16:35,473
So you have to enter into dharma.

145
00:16:35,593 --> 00:16:39,135
Just by birth, you don't really become Buddhist.

146
00:16:41,897 --> 00:16:42,698
Then similarly,

147
00:16:46,890 --> 00:16:54,052
To become Buddhist, I think you have to understand what Buddha is teaching.

148
00:16:57,313 --> 00:17:07,916
And in order to do that, quite often we emphasize so much on culture and tradition.

149
00:17:14,358 --> 00:17:19,502
it is like which holds the teaching.

150
00:17:21,204 --> 00:17:34,715
Or if you look at many of our traditions and cultures, it is somehow connected with, most of the time, it is connected with the Buddhism or teaching of Buddha.

151
00:17:35,675 --> 00:17:50,721
even like Tibetan song, dance, Himalayan song and dance, the old traditional song and dance, it's all something about praising the Buddha or praising Guru Rinpoche.

152
00:17:51,141 --> 00:17:52,822
So somehow it's connected.

153
00:17:53,982 --> 00:17:54,662
So therefore...

154
00:17:57,945 --> 00:18:13,783
we emphasize especially the younger generation to pay more attention towards our own culture and tradition especially i think the language are very important

155
00:18:17,923 --> 00:18:27,149
We talk about not being able to preserve our culture and language in a place where there is no freedom.

156
00:18:28,290 --> 00:18:29,370
We always complain.

157
00:18:29,731 --> 00:18:36,015
But what about those of us who live in a place where there is freedom?

158
00:18:37,416 --> 00:18:41,178
Then I think we should not forget how important it is.

159
00:18:43,477 --> 00:18:47,419
because it's not just culture and tradition.

160
00:18:48,079 --> 00:18:54,142
I think it holds a lot of very important information.

161
00:18:59,868 --> 00:19:13,920
Just by wearing Tibetan dress or just by wearing Himalayan, Bhutanese dress does not make you a genuine kind of holder of the culture and tradition.

162
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:20,585
I think the outer appearance is just a symbol.

163
00:19:21,906 --> 00:19:24,548
The inner Buddhist value

164
00:19:26,069 --> 00:19:31,513
If it is not there, then I think outer dress is just like a show.

165
00:19:32,594 --> 00:19:51,286
So therefore, of course, it is nice to see the outer dress, traditional dress, costumes, but more importantly, the one who is wearing these costumes must uphold the Buddhist value and understand what is Buddhist value.

166
00:19:53,055 --> 00:20:01,984
And just if it's any culture or any language, maybe it's not that important.

167
00:20:03,425 --> 00:20:17,999
In the world, I think we can say we have more than 7,000 different languages, out of which every year we are losing about 10 languages every year.

168
00:20:19,522 --> 00:20:35,259
It's sad, but every language does not have that much information or material to study or read, written in language, like what you call literature.

169
00:20:40,713 --> 00:20:54,424
If you look in the Tibetan literature, which is now used in all Himalaya, not only Tibet, Bhutan and all Himalaya, Tibetan literature is one of the clearly...

170
00:21:00,535 --> 00:21:14,862
literature which holds the information or teaching of Buddha, among the Buddhist literature, let's say, among the Buddhist literature, classic Buddhist literature, we can easily say that the Tibetan language is number one.

171
00:21:16,613 --> 00:21:18,034
Second, Chinese.

172
00:21:18,815 --> 00:21:20,456
Third, Sanskrit.

173
00:21:21,297 --> 00:21:22,378
Fourth, Pali.

174
00:21:23,058 --> 00:21:24,419
Sixth, in Japanese.

175
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:37,549
So we had the maximum material literature written in Tibetan literature from the 7th century till now.

176
00:21:38,010 --> 00:21:40,331
There were so many books and so many materials.

177
00:21:41,531 --> 00:21:57,723
So it will be a pity that our next generation, future generation, younger Himalayan kids, if they don't know how to read, if they don't know how to understand, then this is a waste, so much material.

178
00:22:00,546 --> 00:22:02,267
Even in the Himalayas these days,

179
00:22:04,130 --> 00:22:30,088
some grandchildren the parents are busy and they leave the grandchildren with a mobile phone and youtube and the children start looking at the youtube mostly english youtubes and then they start speaking english before they can start speak native language and then grandmother don't understand what the grandchildren are saying

180
00:22:34,775 --> 00:22:37,016
My grandchildren are speaking ghost language.

181
00:22:38,396 --> 00:22:39,417
So this is happening.

182
00:22:39,917 --> 00:22:43,738
They start speaking English before they can speak their native language.

183
00:22:45,859 --> 00:22:54,502
So I think very important at home, if you try to speak Tibetan, so that the children can learn.

184
00:22:56,003 --> 00:23:02,485
And of course now here there are some facilities to learn Tibetan language.

185
00:23:03,306 --> 00:23:09,888
The reason why I am saying that is the information that we have in the Buddhist literature.

186
00:23:10,848 --> 00:23:15,770
It is very, very useful for future generations.

187
00:23:17,391 --> 00:23:20,132
Because we live in a kind of a world

188
00:23:21,606 --> 00:23:24,968
very much uncertainty, uncertainty.

189
00:23:26,248 --> 00:23:29,890
We don't know what's going to happen, what's coming.

190
00:23:32,451 --> 00:23:37,973
We can think, think back 20 years ago, think back 20 years ago.

191
00:23:39,274 --> 00:23:46,137
Did any of us imagine that everybody will be walking around with a smartphone in their pocket?

192
00:23:47,138 --> 00:23:47,338
No.

193
00:23:48,920 --> 00:23:53,081
When we saw the fax machine, we were amazed, wow, you can get a fax machine.

194
00:23:54,141 --> 00:23:58,102
Now we are walking around with two terabytes in our pocket.

195
00:24:00,502 --> 00:24:03,502
We did not predict that 20 years ago.

196
00:24:05,303 --> 00:24:11,904
We have more computer memory in this than the American rocket went to the moon.

197
00:24:13,284 --> 00:24:14,004
Can you imagine?

198
00:24:14,724 --> 00:24:16,665
And what's going to happen in 20 years?

199
00:24:17,605 --> 00:24:18,185
We don't know.

200
00:24:19,924 --> 00:24:20,764
We have no clue.

201
00:24:21,445 --> 00:24:23,426
Politicians have no idea, I think.

202
00:24:24,386 --> 00:24:25,847
Business people, no idea.

203
00:24:27,688 --> 00:24:32,850
Something going so fast towards future, not knowing where we're going.

204
00:24:34,931 --> 00:24:39,973
It's almost like driving as fast as you can on a highway.

205
00:24:40,913 --> 00:24:42,334
You don't know what's in front of you.

206
00:24:46,076 --> 00:24:46,296
So,

207
00:24:48,193 --> 00:24:56,918
How can we educate our future younger generation how to live their life in 20 years?

208
00:24:57,858 --> 00:24:59,960
We don't know what's going to happen in 20 years.

209
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:06,043
But no matter if they're still alive,

210
00:25:07,929 --> 00:25:18,192
No matter what kind of world, what kind of situation they live, they will always face a problem or challenge with their negative emotions.

211
00:25:19,772 --> 00:25:31,056
They will always have a difficult dealing with their anger, difficult dealing with their jealousy, difficult dealing with their pride.

212
00:25:32,636 --> 00:25:35,237
So if we can invest in them,

213
00:25:37,229 --> 00:25:48,615
Using the Buddhist teaching, how to deal with these negative emotions when you face this situation due to negative emotions.

214
00:25:49,596 --> 00:25:57,320
That is the Buddha's teaching, and that, I think, is the best investment we can put in the children's mind.

215
00:26:02,963 --> 00:26:05,044
I think, of course, we have to...

216
00:26:06,725 --> 00:26:14,127
send the children to school, learn mathematics, science, I don't know, geography, biography, whatever we have to.

217
00:26:15,527 --> 00:26:25,070
But more importantly, I think, the development, inner development, teaching them basic, simple meditation.

218
00:26:25,730 --> 00:26:33,893
slowly, slowly introduce them how to deal with negative emotions, which is the essence of the Buddha's teaching.

219
00:26:34,473 --> 00:26:42,415
That is, I think, the best investment we can do to our future generation.

220
00:26:44,456 --> 00:27:02,363
And that's why it is very pity that if we lose our language, our culture, culture might change, but most importantly, the teaching, the Buddha's teaching.

221
00:27:08,006 --> 00:27:13,188
And also, the teaching of Buddha is not

222
00:27:14,292 --> 00:27:20,896
just a kind of philosophy or idea, or is not ...

223
00:27:20,916 --> 00:27:27,781
I don't think we can really say it is purely a kind of religion, believing in something.

224
00:27:30,923 --> 00:27:36,567
Buddha's teaching, I think, is based on fact, based on reality.

225
00:27:39,456 --> 00:27:48,321
Example, if someone is not well, if you don't feel well, then first you say, oh, I'm not feeling well.

226
00:27:49,142 --> 00:27:54,926
Then second, you want to go and see a doctor because you're not feeling well.

227
00:27:55,806 --> 00:27:57,087
Then you go to see a doctor.

228
00:27:58,308 --> 00:28:00,609
Doctor examines you.

229
00:28:01,009 --> 00:28:02,290
Oh, something is wrong with you.

230
00:28:02,970 --> 00:28:04,251
Identifies the sickness.

231
00:28:05,552 --> 00:28:06,873
Then gives you the prescription.

232
00:28:10,224 --> 00:28:11,005
give you the diet.

233
00:28:13,447 --> 00:28:14,608
So then you follow that.

234
00:28:15,669 --> 00:28:18,952
If you follow that, then you will be cured from this sickness.

235
00:28:21,274 --> 00:28:29,861
So that kind of whole process, I don't think we can call it a religion or neither philosophy.

236
00:28:31,644 --> 00:28:44,671
Someone getting sick, first understanding I'm sick, then you don't want to be sick, you go to see a doctor, doctor give prescription, you follow the prescription, and you're free from the sickness.

237
00:28:45,492 --> 00:28:47,713
So that process, we don't call it religion.

238
00:28:48,614 --> 00:28:50,795
Or this process, we don't call it philosophy.

239
00:28:52,096 --> 00:28:57,779
So similarly, all the Buddha's teaching from

240
00:29:00,339 --> 00:29:03,700
Shravakayana all the way to Vajrayana.

241
00:29:04,861 --> 00:29:14,404
It is within the framework of what we call the Four Noble Truths, which is very similar to what I have just mentioned.

242
00:29:15,645 --> 00:29:28,009
First is the Dungaljit Demba, Truth of the Suffering, Truth of the Suffering in this world.

243
00:29:31,251 --> 00:29:35,354
a lot of dissatisfaction in the life.

244
00:29:37,175 --> 00:29:46,140
Normally, a classic example is Kayga Nachi, the suffering of birth, suffering of sickness, suffering of old age, suffering of death.

245
00:29:47,081 --> 00:29:55,246
So somehow, no matter who we are, whether you are powerful, rich, poor, we are within that cycle of this suffering.

246
00:29:59,357 --> 00:30:00,697
What causes the suffering?

247
00:30:01,517 --> 00:30:02,098
Second thing.

248
00:30:03,118 --> 00:30:04,338
What causes the suffering?

249
00:30:05,058 --> 00:30:06,578
We call it Kuinjungi Denpa.

250
00:30:07,659 --> 00:30:09,159
The suffering has a cause.

251
00:30:10,019 --> 00:30:21,001
Like example, if you are sick, the doctor will say, oh, you got sick because you eat too much chili or maybe ice cream.

252
00:30:23,741 --> 00:30:25,102
So what causes the suffering?

253
00:30:25,322 --> 00:30:26,502
The cause of the suffering

254
00:30:28,277 --> 00:30:33,220
Desire, anger, jealousy, hatred, they are negative.

255
00:30:33,240 --> 00:30:37,503
Because nobody feels very happy when they are angry.

256
00:30:38,403 --> 00:30:41,425
Nobody is very happy when they are jealous.

257
00:30:42,766 --> 00:30:47,229
Nobody wakes up in the morning and says, may I suffer today?

258
00:30:48,029 --> 00:30:48,970
Nobody wants suffering.

259
00:30:49,710 --> 00:30:52,732
So what causes the suffering, if you sort of analyze it?

260
00:30:54,160 --> 00:30:59,222
Everything leads back to our own negative emotions, anger, jealousy, hatred.

261
00:30:59,402 --> 00:31:07,825
Of course, among all, the ignorance, not understanding the truth, not understanding the truth of reality.

262
00:31:11,026 --> 00:31:19,209
Then the third question, Gopi Denpa, can the suffering be stopped?

263
00:31:19,549 --> 00:31:20,990
Can the suffering be stopped?

264
00:31:24,053 --> 00:31:29,137
Yes, because the cause of the suffering is negative emotions.

265
00:31:29,737 --> 00:31:35,582
It is not really inherent to the nature of oneself.

266
00:31:37,423 --> 00:31:44,208
It is not inherent.

267
00:31:45,669 --> 00:31:50,553
So, for example, if your cup is dirty,

268
00:31:52,716 --> 00:31:54,317
The dirty is on the cup.

269
00:31:54,517 --> 00:31:56,598
That doesn't mean the cup itself is dirty.

270
00:31:57,238 --> 00:31:58,439
You can wash away the cup.

271
00:31:59,319 --> 00:32:05,302
Similarly, the negative emotions is not inherent, is not the nature of yourself.

272
00:32:06,542 --> 00:32:12,605
So there are many kind of teaching and the logic explanation behind that.

273
00:32:15,575 --> 00:32:20,696
So if it is not inherited, then we can remove it.

274
00:32:21,337 --> 00:32:22,897
There is a possibility to remove.

275
00:32:25,838 --> 00:32:30,119
Then the fourth teaching comes, how to remove that, how to remove that suffering.

276
00:32:32,799 --> 00:32:39,341
Then there comes all the Buddha's teachings, different methods, different paths,

277
00:32:43,872 --> 00:32:47,473
Like Theravada tradition, they have their own method.

278
00:32:49,594 --> 00:32:52,415
Mahayana tradition, another different method.

279
00:32:53,255 --> 00:32:56,076
And Vajrayana, different methods.

280
00:32:57,457 --> 00:33:12,542
So even though the different Buddhist schools look different from outwardly, Theravada's way they dress, how they do different, Mahayana you see different, Vajrayana you see different, but it's a different path.

281
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:18,662
to get rid of the cause of the suffering.

282
00:33:19,402 --> 00:33:25,105
So these are very much based on reality, based on the fact.

283
00:33:25,965 --> 00:33:31,348
Just like the example I have given, someone feeling sick, what is the cause of the sickness?

284
00:33:32,489 --> 00:33:37,331
And first accepting the sickness, I think it is very important.

285
00:33:39,127 --> 00:33:49,174
If you think everything is okay, everything is fine, then it doesn't sort of make you go to see a doctor or seek a solution.

286
00:33:49,994 --> 00:33:57,699
So first thing, I think the samsaric world, everything is impermanent, everything is changing.

287
00:33:59,380 --> 00:34:03,763
So at the end, everything brings suffering.

288
00:34:06,067 --> 00:34:09,268
So then we seek for a kind of a solution.

289
00:34:09,828 --> 00:34:15,289
So I think the basic Four Noble Truths, if you can understand what Buddha's teaching is.

290
00:34:18,409 --> 00:34:31,412
And then one thing very clearly Buddha mentions is, analyze my teaching and don't take it for granted.

291
00:34:33,269 --> 00:34:38,031
So therefore, we are, let's say, we are like authorized.

292
00:34:39,711 --> 00:34:42,792
We have the right to analyze the Buddha's teaching.

293
00:34:44,133 --> 00:34:56,137
And that, I think, no other faith or the religious school have this kind of message.

294
00:34:58,523 --> 00:35:05,389
As far as what I know, most of the faith say, you must believe because someone says so.

295
00:35:07,010 --> 00:35:08,011
Buddha never said that.

296
00:35:08,091 --> 00:35:09,773
Buddha said, analyze my teaching.

297
00:35:10,293 --> 00:35:11,434
Don't take it for granted.

298
00:35:12,495 --> 00:35:14,117
So this is a very, very powerful.

299
00:35:16,577 --> 00:35:18,098
that we should understand.

300
00:35:18,238 --> 00:35:21,301
Sometimes we don't understand, we just take it for granted.

301
00:35:22,141 --> 00:35:28,906
But no, I think when you look at many other schools, many other faiths, it is not like that.

302
00:35:29,887 --> 00:35:42,696
So this teaching, very precious teaching of Buddha, and nowadays a lot of the

303
00:35:45,436 --> 00:35:54,106
people who are not background, not born as a Buddhist, but they are getting interest.

304
00:35:57,158 --> 00:36:03,846
Of course, we are not going around knocking on the people's door and trying to convert people.

305
00:36:04,827 --> 00:36:12,316
But, of course, if they read, it makes sense, then people, when they come, seek the teaching.

306
00:36:12,897 --> 00:36:15,680
Then it is our duty to share with them.

307
00:36:21,667 --> 00:36:33,738
And one thing that His Holiness the Dalai Lama quite often and very much emphasized is Buddhism and science.

308
00:36:42,447 --> 00:36:52,770
Because I think we live in kind of time where anything which is scientifically proven, then we're ready to jump in it.

309
00:36:55,451 --> 00:37:00,152
You remember not so long ago, we have this COVID.

310
00:37:02,053 --> 00:37:10,395
As soon as we have the vaccine saying that scientifically proven, everybody roll up their sleeve and got injection.

311
00:37:13,548 --> 00:37:14,488
ready to jump in it.

312
00:37:14,828 --> 00:37:24,212
So, I think if it says scientifically proven, then everybody sort of takes that as, oh, that's the truth.

313
00:37:26,033 --> 00:37:29,394
More than Buddha's words, a scientist's words.

314
00:37:30,314 --> 00:37:41,178
So, with seeing this, I think His Holiness quite often mentions that science, Buddhism, dialogue between science and Buddhism,

315
00:37:43,217 --> 00:37:44,377
emphasizes in that.

316
00:37:45,058 --> 00:37:52,920
And there have been many meetings and many kinds of, I think we can say, experiments done.

317
00:37:55,520 --> 00:38:07,924
And I am also very much interested, even though I did not go to regular school to learn those things, but in 2003, the beginning of this kind of research,

318
00:38:11,945 --> 00:38:20,009
I participated in some of the experiments as a guinea pig.

319
00:38:20,709 --> 00:38:25,992
Not that I am a good meditator or anything, but I just wanted to explore.

320
00:38:27,427 --> 00:38:32,448
And there is one thing which interests me a lot.

321
00:38:32,468 --> 00:38:35,589
There is one experiment they did.

322
00:38:36,970 --> 00:38:38,350
I think it was in Los Angeles in 2003.

323
00:38:38,490 --> 00:38:44,011
One day they said that everybody has their kind of

324
00:38:52,074 --> 00:38:52,354
Sorry?

325
00:38:53,275 --> 00:38:55,397
Baseline or attitude, right?

326
00:38:55,537 --> 00:38:55,977
Attitude?

327
00:38:56,798 --> 00:38:58,019
Attitude, kind of attitude.

328
00:38:59,800 --> 00:39:09,307
Like, some people are naturally a little bit optimistic.

329
00:39:09,587 --> 00:39:14,471
I'm sure you have friends, no matter where you go, who always say, oh, it's cool.

330
00:39:14,931 --> 00:39:19,074
Even if it's a funky kind of house, you say, this is cool.

331
00:39:21,872 --> 00:39:26,015
I think some of the architects who built those towers must have been very positive architects.

332
00:39:26,756 --> 00:39:30,739
When I saw this building on the way, I thought something is wrong.

333
00:39:31,460 --> 00:39:32,361
It was all crooked.

334
00:39:34,943 --> 00:39:38,686
And somebody told me, a very famous architect purposely did that.

335
00:39:39,346 --> 00:39:39,627
Anyway.

336
00:39:41,925 --> 00:39:44,828
If it's in Nepal, you will say, bad architect.

337
00:39:45,889 --> 00:39:48,311
In Canada, you say, oh, it's a beautiful art.

338
00:39:50,653 --> 00:39:55,398
Anyway, everybody has their own kind of baseline.

339
00:39:55,498 --> 00:39:58,841
Some people naturally see, oh, it's cool, it's nice.

340
00:40:00,362 --> 00:40:07,148
And some people naturally, wherever you go, always feel something is wrong, a little bit pessimistic.

341
00:40:08,129 --> 00:40:12,774
So everybody has their own baseline.

342
00:40:14,155 --> 00:40:16,357
And then there are ups and downs.

343
00:40:17,218 --> 00:40:17,878
Like when you ...

344
00:40:23,122 --> 00:40:36,625
When you receive, let's say, a lot of money, lottery ticket, you receive a lot of money or something, and then your baseline is here, and your happiness shoots up, goes up.

345
00:40:38,205 --> 00:40:41,886
Within six months or one year, it comes down to baseline.

346
00:40:42,826 --> 00:40:43,766
It doesn't stay there.

347
00:40:44,646 --> 00:40:47,627
If you're always, your baseline stays there.

348
00:40:51,332 --> 00:41:00,220
And then the love at first sight goes up.

349
00:41:02,261 --> 00:41:06,105
Then, after six months, it comes down to the baseline.

350
00:41:06,825 --> 00:41:08,427
After marriage, it goes below.

351
00:41:12,546 --> 00:41:19,391
immediate family die or something terrible happen, goes down, comes back to baseline.

352
00:41:20,631 --> 00:41:22,753
So they say baseline is always there.

353
00:41:23,893 --> 00:41:39,584
But what they found through this research is that by contemplating on kind of meditation like loving kindness or four boundless loves, the baseline goes up.

354
00:41:40,724 --> 00:41:41,705
Baseline goes up.

355
00:41:43,190 --> 00:41:48,694
You become more positive, you become happier, you become more optimistic.

356
00:41:49,855 --> 00:41:52,197
So this they could see in the machine.

357
00:41:54,919 --> 00:42:00,843
I wasn't a good example, but even we had to go through the MRI machine, hook up to the computer.

358
00:42:00,883 --> 00:42:02,004
It was 2003.

359
00:42:02,445 --> 00:42:05,387
Now they have a lot more information, I'm sure.

360
00:42:05,407 --> 00:42:09,010
But in those days, they already mentioned that.

361
00:42:09,770 --> 00:42:12,852
So these are kind of ...

362
00:42:13,253 --> 00:42:30,424
I'm sure when you do a lot of practice, maybe you feel, maybe you don't really feel, but when you put it in the machine and analyze it, when you actually can see, then it's really kind of believing.

363
00:42:32,255 --> 00:42:53,489
So these are the Buddhist methods, the scientists, they do research, and they did many, many, after, I think, they have a lot of information that how the Buddhist teaching, the technique, meditation, can really make changes to our brain.

364
00:42:56,707 --> 00:42:59,469
become a better person, become a happier person.

365
00:43:00,589 --> 00:43:06,292
They say it's almost like the muscle, mental muscle.

366
00:43:07,213 --> 00:43:12,015
When you go to the gym, you do a lot of exercise, your muscle becomes stronger.

367
00:43:12,756 --> 00:43:24,682
So similarly, we train our mind to be optimistic by using the techniques, especially like Lob Chong Mind Training, For Boundless Love,

368
00:43:26,151 --> 00:43:30,163
And then your mind becomes better, so you can see all those things.

369
00:43:34,858 --> 00:43:37,039
So I thought that was very interesting.

370
00:43:37,659 --> 00:43:56,206
Quite often I share in my Dharma talks I give, and always, always, no matter what country, what language you speak, when you say, the happiness goes below the baseline after marriage, it gives a big laugh.

371
00:44:00,728 --> 00:44:02,689
So this thing is kind of real evidence

372
00:44:04,832 --> 00:44:29,504
Answer to the earlier question for the young Himalayan boy is not that what our ancestors, what the previous masters, what they've been doing in the hermitage, what they've been practicing is something relevant in our world, something really can benefit not only us,

373
00:44:30,845 --> 00:44:34,908
But even now we are able to share with the rest of the world and many people.

374
00:44:35,548 --> 00:44:42,993
One of the conferences, a long time ago, around that time, 2003, His Holiness gave a big conference in MIT.

375
00:44:43,033 --> 00:44:46,415
There were about 800 neuroscientists who participated.

376
00:44:55,201 --> 00:45:04,992
So this shows that how much information, how much sort of advance the teaching

377
00:45:12,480 --> 00:45:25,106
So I think this is important for our younger generation to know that how important, how much information we hold in the Tibetan texts, Tibetan languages.

378
00:45:25,986 --> 00:45:40,813
So if we can, the next generation, people really have to understand their value and preserve our tradition and value.

379
00:45:49,174 --> 00:46:00,203
I will just give you a small kind of basic meditation for those who are interested.

380
00:46:01,064 --> 00:46:04,287
We call it something called bodhicitta.

381
00:46:05,067 --> 00:46:13,795
In Tibetan, we call it jangchubgi sem, bodhicitta mind, generating love and compassion, training.

382
00:46:14,415 --> 00:46:16,297
There is something called four boundless love.

383
00:46:21,300 --> 00:46:34,123
So first, like the His Holiness's four point mentions, everybody wish to be happy, not to be suffer.

384
00:46:34,603 --> 00:46:37,084
Everybody has a right to be happy.

385
00:46:38,324 --> 00:46:46,946
So therefore, with understanding that, then we developed our kind of loving kindness towards all sentient beings.

386
00:46:47,847 --> 00:46:49,347
So it's just simple thinking.

387
00:46:51,759 --> 00:46:53,620
Some people will say, oh, why we should care?

388
00:46:53,820 --> 00:46:57,462
Why we should generate loving kindness to others?

389
00:47:00,303 --> 00:47:13,308
If you think, every kind of what we call relative happiness, even relative happiness, relative kind of joy, it all depends on others.

390
00:47:14,969 --> 00:47:21,012
Example, the place that we are using here, it is all built by others.

391
00:47:23,778 --> 00:47:29,641
Without this, we will have to sit outside in the sun, in the rain.

392
00:47:30,501 --> 00:47:33,663
So, feel grateful to others.

393
00:47:34,523 --> 00:47:38,505
Similarly, the clothes we wear are made by others.

394
00:47:40,346 --> 00:47:43,147
If the others did not make, what are we going to wear?

395
00:47:44,817 --> 00:47:46,420
So, feel grateful to others.

396
00:47:47,542 --> 00:47:52,210
Everything that we consider gives us comfort and happiness,

397
00:47:56,056 --> 00:47:56,937
It's due to others.

398
00:47:58,137 --> 00:48:00,178
So that's how we have to train.

399
00:48:00,198 --> 00:48:03,720
Because someone might think, oh, I paid for it.

400
00:48:04,801 --> 00:48:06,942
I worked, I made money, I paid for it.

401
00:48:07,022 --> 00:48:11,104
But if they did not create, then we cannot buy.

402
00:48:12,044 --> 00:48:19,888
Imagine yourself alone in this land, Canada.

403
00:48:20,509 --> 00:48:21,809
You're alone by yourself.

404
00:48:23,470 --> 00:48:24,951
Imagine without others.

405
00:48:27,083 --> 00:48:33,046
So like that, thinking, appreciating others, what people have created.

406
00:48:33,106 --> 00:48:40,469
So every kind of happiness, every kind of comfort, it's all due to others, the food we eat.

407
00:48:42,090 --> 00:48:44,451
So this kind of gratitude we've built up.

408
00:48:45,232 --> 00:48:48,933
That's how we train our mind, first step, fielding gratitude.

409
00:48:50,274 --> 00:48:56,597
And then classic Tibetan kind of teaching says that all sentient beings are our mother,

410
00:48:58,707 --> 00:49:15,933
Mother, the reason why we choose mother is because we have been taking nine months in the mother's womb, and after birth we are fed, we are taught how to walk, we are taught how to eat.

411
00:49:16,913 --> 00:49:19,034
Without mother, we will not survive.

412
00:49:21,184 --> 00:49:22,144
So thinking like that.

413
00:49:22,965 --> 00:49:34,429
And then, this kindness of this mother, when you feel strong kind of compassion, then we slowly think, this is not only one lifetime.

414
00:49:34,709 --> 00:49:36,449
We have many, many, many lifetimes.

415
00:49:37,229 --> 00:49:39,570
Every birth, there's always a mother.

416
00:49:40,511 --> 00:49:47,753
So then we sort of, a little bit, a broader mind, all sentient beings being our mother, kind parents.

417
00:49:48,533 --> 00:49:49,734
So that's how we train.

418
00:49:51,415 --> 00:49:57,399
Then after that, when you feel strong kind of loving-kindness, then we shift.

419
00:49:57,959 --> 00:50:09,547
We can shift towards equal all sentient beings, even though in this life we feel like not friendly person or enemy.

420
00:50:10,307 --> 00:50:12,609
But in past life, they must be our parents.

421
00:50:13,950 --> 00:50:15,391
Tangnyom, economity.

422
00:50:16,992 --> 00:50:18,793
And then we shift, focus.

423
00:50:21,561 --> 00:50:22,704
on what we call Nying-Jie.

424
00:50:23,908 --> 00:50:26,155
There are many beings suffering in this world

425
00:50:29,129 --> 00:50:33,191
due to sickness, due to hunger, so many suffering.

426
00:50:33,331 --> 00:50:37,733
We just turn on the television, everything always there's suffering.

427
00:50:38,894 --> 00:50:42,415
So thinking they're also these beings who are suffering.

428
00:50:43,336 --> 00:50:48,578
And one time they've been our parents, they've been our loved ones, they've been our kind of family.

429
00:50:49,479 --> 00:50:51,920
And then feeling strong kind of compassion.

430
00:50:53,882 --> 00:50:57,064
Slowly shift, shift this kind of mind.

431
00:50:57,125 --> 00:51:07,032
When you feel a little bit down, you feel like depressed, feel a little bit like hopelessness, then we shift our focus to rejoice.

432
00:51:08,553 --> 00:51:18,621
You're seeing people who has good life, people who has engaged in dharma, who has been practicing dharma, so we shift that.

433
00:51:19,061 --> 00:51:21,503
And then you feel uplifted, rejoice.

434
00:51:23,193 --> 00:51:34,680
And then if you sometimes, because our mind can be tricky, and it becomes like jumpy, or too much excitement, then again we shift to the equanimity.

435
00:51:34,700 --> 00:51:44,826
So the four-bounded love is a beautiful teaching by Jigme Lingpa in Treasury of Spiritual Qualities.

436
00:51:45,467 --> 00:51:50,930
Very beautiful method, very simple, shifting these four-bounded love.

437
00:51:51,871 --> 00:51:52,351
Jamba,

438
00:51:58,276 --> 00:51:59,978
Very simple, very, very helpful.

439
00:52:01,579 --> 00:52:07,464
So like that, this is kind of basic training, what we call Bodhicitta or Jangchubsem.

440
00:52:10,069 --> 00:52:27,799
So real bodhicitta is a possibility, understanding the possibility that all sentient beings can be free from the suffering of samsara, and then wish that may all be free from the suffering of samsara.

441
00:52:28,199 --> 00:52:29,860
So that is what we call the bodhicitta.

442
00:52:33,705 --> 00:52:40,309
This training, I think we can do, if you want to do daily practice, very simple, very helpful.

443
00:52:40,889 --> 00:52:42,990
You don't need so many objects.

444
00:52:44,391 --> 00:52:47,513
Just sit quietly and contemplate this.

445
00:52:47,733 --> 00:52:50,114
And this is the essence of all the teachings.

446
00:52:50,694 --> 00:52:56,938
No matter what school you follow, all will, I think, speak the same.

447
00:52:58,939 --> 00:53:05,862
And then post-meditation, I think we have to kind of gradually improve.

448
00:53:08,003 --> 00:53:09,704
Gradually improve ourselves.

449
00:53:11,364 --> 00:53:19,648
Not that we just make a prayer, offer some lamps, give some money, and then go back to normal life.

450
00:53:20,488 --> 00:53:23,870
We don't try to improve ourselves gradually.

451
00:53:23,950 --> 00:53:26,391
Then it doesn't really work.

452
00:53:33,092 --> 00:53:36,274
I often joke about my weight.

453
00:53:37,535 --> 00:53:38,275
I'm quite heavy.

454
00:53:38,515 --> 00:53:39,215
I'm quite fat.

455
00:53:39,776 --> 00:53:41,177
I wasn't so fat like this.

456
00:53:42,557 --> 00:53:52,122
But I cannot go to a temple and light a lamp and offer some money saying, may I become thin, may I become thin, may I become thin.

457
00:53:52,863 --> 00:53:54,063
Om, I'm thin soha.

458
00:53:54,163 --> 00:53:55,084
Om, I'm thin soha.

459
00:53:56,045 --> 00:53:58,726
It's not going to lose weight for sure.

460
00:53:59,586 --> 00:54:01,367
If it's possible, I would have tried.

461
00:54:04,330 --> 00:54:06,533
So of course, you make aspiration.

462
00:54:08,095 --> 00:54:09,497
We have a lot of diet books.

463
00:54:11,635 --> 00:54:36,432
in your shell if you don't implement them you're not going to lose weight so i have my attendant trying to control my potions in the food he's not doing a good job i'm not losing so much weight anyway you know you try to eat little bit less you try to walk little more

464
00:54:40,494 --> 00:54:42,195
Then you might try to lose weight, right?

465
00:54:42,775 --> 00:54:51,080
So similarly, I think to improve ourselves, to generate more loving kindness, we have to start from a small.

466
00:54:52,541 --> 00:54:57,563
We just can't just small thing, we just ignore saying, oh, there's something big I'm going to do.

467
00:54:59,344 --> 00:55:00,685
Then maybe it will not work.

468
00:55:02,208 --> 00:55:16,084
For example, I know many of you are driving an Uber, or driving in the ... Maybe Canada is a little bit better at following rules.

469
00:55:17,046 --> 00:55:17,927
Imagine New York.

470
00:55:19,148 --> 00:55:21,611
Everybody is shouting at each other when they are driving.

471
00:55:22,632 --> 00:55:26,054
Anybody who drives faster than you is crazy.

472
00:55:27,195 --> 00:55:29,697
Anybody who drives slower than you is stupid.

473
00:55:31,098 --> 00:55:33,119
So everybody has to follow your rhythm.

474
00:55:34,940 --> 00:55:37,402
Or somebody goes zoom, oh, he's a crazy guy.

475
00:55:38,383 --> 00:55:40,284
Somebody is slow in front, oh, stupid guy.

476
00:55:42,205 --> 00:55:44,307
So I think like that.

477
00:55:45,608 --> 00:55:47,289
That is kind of anger, right?

478
00:55:47,789 --> 00:55:50,252
So I think try to be patient.

479
00:55:50,813 --> 00:55:52,055
That's maybe a small step.

480
00:55:53,176 --> 00:56:04,370
Similarly, there are many opportunities in our life where we can practice to become a better human being by not cursing the other driver.

481
00:56:05,792 --> 00:56:21,540
i don't know see somebody like other day i was saying you're waiting for a bus and there are a lot of people waiting you don't have to go and kiss and hug everybody but you just sit there and mentally you wish that person be happy

482
00:56:22,817 --> 00:56:23,397
Just wish.

483
00:56:23,918 --> 00:56:24,718
It's very simple.

484
00:56:25,959 --> 00:56:35,604
And small, small, I think in the teaching it says, The small spark of fire can burn the whole, what do you call, haste.

485
00:56:41,227 --> 00:56:52,980
So similarly, when the anger or hatred or desire, when it's small, when it grows, you have to first, you have to put down.

486
00:56:54,221 --> 00:56:55,843
Then the big fire will not come.

487
00:56:57,104 --> 00:57:03,408
We ignore the small spark, and then by the time it's a big fire, then we cannot control.

488
00:57:04,569 --> 00:57:05,149
So like that.

489
00:57:05,510 --> 00:57:14,415
Of course, we make a cumulative merit, we offer lamps, we offer the water offering, but at the same time, we have to work.

490
00:57:15,696 --> 00:57:19,799
Daily life, day to day, when you go to work, when you go to office,

491
00:57:20,579 --> 00:57:28,510
And anything negative emotion, first recognize, and then there are so many different methods to deal with this.

492
00:57:28,930 --> 00:57:37,802
From small we deal, and then when it never becomes big, and then we improve ourselves, become a better human being.

493
00:57:39,104 --> 00:57:45,365
And I think facing life also becomes easier like that.

494
00:57:45,486 --> 00:57:48,526
So I thought to share this with you.

495
00:57:50,987 --> 00:58:04,330
And then, after this, today is the Menzushiri Empowerment from Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo's Circle of Teaching.

496
00:58:08,620 --> 00:58:13,245
I received this many times from Chabjit Tengku Chinzirinpoche.

497
00:58:13,265 --> 00:58:18,931
So today I will bestow the...

498
00:58:19,132 --> 00:58:29,443
Since there are a lot of younger generation who wants to receive the mensushiri empowerment, so today I will give this mensushiri empowerment.

499
00:59:28,708 --> 00:59:29,049
Thank you.

